sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch04-04> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Seemingly trivial characters such as colour can be shaped by natural selection; colours that match the background, as in green leaf-eating insects and heather-coloured grouse, protect their bearers from predators that hunt by sight.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Protective colours matching backgrounds are maintained by selection";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter IV, “Natural Selection”; section “Natural Selection: its power compared with man’s selection, its power on characters of trifling importance, its power at all ages and on both sexes”. Scope: animals preyed on by visually hunting predators"
}
